SSH - Secure Shell.

-- A Remote management tool. Suite of binaries [ ssh sshd ssh-add ssh-agent ssh-copy-id ].
sudo pacman -S openssh
-- SSH protocol implementation for remote login, command execution and file transfer.
-- Depends on OpenSSL - toolkit for SSL and TLS.
-- Listens to port 22 by default.

-- Unit file operations.
systemctl <enable/disable/start/stop/restart> sshd

Service file @: /usr/lib/systemd/system/sshd.service
Config files @: ~/.ssh/
~/.ssh/authorized_keys - A file that contains the public of the clients.

-- In the client.
Running the ssh-agent and adding the key, can help in remote login without passphrases being asked again and again. 

-- Connecting to a remote server.
ssh -p <port_number|d:22> user@remote_host_name_or_ip
Changing the port number is a simple security increment. 


-- SSH Client Binary.
SSH client - /usr/bin/ssh
An ssh client allows you to connect to a remote server and not the other way around.
Remote server has a host-key[fingerprint]

It's stored in the known_hosts file.
To avoid MITM attacks.

/var/log/auth.log to troubleshoot ssh login.

-- Configuring SSH client.
touch ~/.ssh/config

Host <foo>
    Hostname <ip addr of the host>
    Port 22
    User <username in the host>
:wq
ssh foo - connect to the host with name 'foo' in the config file.

-- SSH keys
Avoid having to type the password.
If you fail the key, will ask the password. But can be disabled.

id_ed25519 id_ed25519.pub - Example of files that contain the public and the private ssh key.

ssh-keygen - creates a public private (rsa) key pair
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your comment/default: clientusername@hostname"

-- Note: Enter a passphrase for a generated key.
The passphrase is asked locally when the private key is used. This way even if you lose the key, they would need the passphrase to actually use it.
    
    Look if you are overwriting the same file. If you do so, you may lose the ability to remotely connect to the server.
    
    If the private key has leaked out. Stop using that is the servers.

Add the public key<client side> to the server-end<host> and it can be used by the host to authenticate that it's you trying to connect remotely. 
If the private key <of client> is the right one for the public key in the remote server, you are logged in.

To put in .ssh/authorized_keys file in the remote server.

ssh -v user@ip

The host looks for match with every private key in the client and if it matches it logs in as the user.

-- Copying the public key of the client into the remote server.
1. Manually copy the keyfile.pub contents into authorized_keys file in the remote.
2. Use ssh-copy-id -i[input file in client] ~/.ssh/inclient.pub user@remote_ip


-- Managing keys.
-- Keys are specific to users. If pushed the public key to the user, you can't login in as root with that key.
-- If you have many clients, it is advised to generate a pair for each client. 

If you give the same public key to every one of your client. If the private key leaks, all of them will have compromised keys.

-- Disabling password authentication and only allowing cryptographic authentication.

Using a specific ssh key for remote login.

ssh -i[input file] ~/.ssh/client_pri_key user@remote_hostname.
-- Since the remote has the public key, it can verify the authenticity of the client.

-- Using ssh-agents
SSH agents are automatically started in most desktop linux distros.
But in case of a bare server/minimal installation, you will have to enable it.

SSH agents allows the client to give the passphrase once and use it for the whole session
i.e until you close the attached terminal.

In the client, perform the follow steps.
eval "$(ssh-agent)" [ confirm by running ps aux | grep ssh-agent ]
ssh-add path_to_private_key - this asks for the passphrase once and then stores it into the memory in client.
  This skips the need of the user to input the passphrase every time. 




-- SSH Server binary.
/usr/bin/sshd - A daemon that runs in the background which accepts connections.

-- In Debian based systems, systemctl status ssh.

Checking the status using systemd[init system] - systemctl status sshd
systemctl stop sshd - Terminates the daemon, but maintains the current connections.

/etc/ssh/ - Has config files, host-keys.
So, if you clone your machine, the host-keys will be cloned with the rest of the filesystem, remember to change them.

/etc/ssh/ssh_config - A configuration file for SSH, the local ~/.ssh/config overrides it.

/etc/ssh/sshd_config - A file that has configurations for how it handles remote connections.
If you add PasswordAuthentication no - Then you have to login using the ssh-agent. It doesn't even allow you to enter passphrase while login.
Also, you can't even push the public key using ssh-copy-id if configured to no.

  -- IMPORTANT: 
    1. PasswordAuthentication no - After you have successfully established a key relationship with the remote server.
    2. Port XYZ - To change the port number.
    3. PermitRootLogin no - If you have a non-root user to use<Recommended>.

-- Troubleshooting OpenSSH
A timeout means that the port has been blocked. [ The firewall just blocks it. No other info is given. ]
For security reasons, the hacker must not know what actually blocked the ssh request
    1. Was the port not listening?
    2. Is the firewall blocking my request?
    3. Am I not able to connect to the internet?
    4. Is the service down?
    5. Is the service running listening to SSH on a different port? 

Permissions in both client and remote server:
    1. Public key readable by anyone. 
    2. Private key only by the owner.
    3. ~/.ssh rwx only for the user.

Log files.
    1. tail -f /var/log/auth.log[debian] - If a user has a problem, tail and troubleshoot only.
    2. Follow journalctl [ systems using systemd ]  
        journalctl -u[unit name] -f[follow] sshd


-- Root
All of roots ssh files can be found in /etc/ssh and root/.ssh

All the files in /etc/ssh is owned by the root.

For the first time you have to enable password auth for ssh, push the public key of the client into authorized_keys.
Then, change to either no/prohibit-password, restart the daemon and then use it to login as the root to the remote server. 
